exports[`Quillfield Automation > Strategy System › 1. Situation & Direction > tile strategy-foundation-0 1`] = `
"Current SituationCurrent SituationiWe sell AI Workflow Audit ($2,500 a project), Automation Build ($7,500 a project) and Automation Care Retainer ($1,200 a month) to NYC Service Firms. Customers find and reach us through LinkedIn, Main Website, Booking Page, Newsletter, Direct Email, Referral Partner, Event / Talk, Invoice, Zoom, Slack, Customer Referral, X (Twitter), Agency founder communities, Contact Page and Demo. We start with $10,700 in hand and are seeking $15,000 (SBA Microloan). At the normal level we expect $14,250 a month in revenue, breaking even in Jul 2027. The plan still has a funding gap of $14,842.RefreshiBack‹›Back
[fields] We sell AI Workflow Audit ($2,500 a project), Automation Build ($7,500 a project) and Automation Care Retainer ($1,200 a month) to NYC Service Firms.
Customers find and reach us through LinkedIn, Main Website, Booking Page, Newsletter, Direct Email, Referral Partner, Event / Talk, Invoice, Zoom, Slack, Customer Referral, X (Twitter), Agency founder communities, Contact Page and Demo.
We start with $10,700 in hand and are seeking $15,000 (SBA Microloan). At the normal level we expect $14,250 a month in revenue, breaking even in Jul 2027. The plan still has a funding gap of $14,842."
`;

exports[`Quillfield Automation > Strategy System › 1. Situation & Direction > tile strategy-foundation-1 1`] = `
"SWOT AnalysisSWOT AnalysisiStrengths1.Founder with hands-on automation and solutions architecture experience2.Three fixed-price services that are easy for a small firm to buy3.A Care Retainer that turns one-off projects into recurring revenue4.Low fixed costs: a coworking desk and no staffWeaknesses1.A single founder's capacity limits how many builds can run at once2.The plan relies on an SBA microloan that is not yet confirmed3.Cash runs short in mid 2027, even with the loan4.No case studies or client track record yetHow could these changes affect you?i12345TechnologyRules & lawThe economySociety & lifestyleSupply & deliveryEnvironment & climateOpportunities1.Small professional firms want to use AI but have no one in-house to set it up2.Automation platforms keep getting cheaper and more capable3.Referrals through accountants and professional associations4.Ongoing maintenance demand as firms add more toolsThreats1.Larger agencies packaging low-cost automation offers for small firms2.AI tools that let firms automate on their own, pushing prices down3.Stricter privacy and professional-conduct rules on the use of AI4.An economic slowdown delaying discretionary projectsBack‹›Back
